EKS Auto Mode — Pod Pending: Root Cause Analysis & Runbook
Incident Summary
Cluster: eks (us-east-1) Impact: All pods stuck in Pending / ContainerCreating for ~2.5 hours Resolution: Attached AmazonEKSNetworkingPolicy to AmazonEKSClusterRole

Root Causes (in order of discovery)
#	Component	Finding	Impact	Severity
1	AmazonEKSClusterRole	AmazonEKSNetworkingPolicy not attached	IPAM controller denied ec2:AssignPrivateIpAddresses — no IPs assigned to pods on any node	Critical
2	EKS Access Entries	Stale AmazonEKSAutoNodeRole1 access entry (wrong type, not EC2_LINUX)	New Auto Mode nodes failed to register with cluster — immediately deprovisioned as Empty	High
3	EKS Addons	vpc-cni addon installed on an Auto Mode cluster	Stale aws-cni binary on nodes conflicted with Auto Mode native CNI — FailedCreatePodSandBox on every pod	High
Error Chain
How the three misconfigurations compounded each other into a 2.5-hour outage:

AmazonEKSNetworkingPolicy missing → IPAM controller denied ec2:AssignPrivateIpAddresses → no pod IPs assigned → all pods Pending
Stale AmazonEKSAutoNodeRole1 access entry → new nodes launched by Auto Mode failed to register → nodes immediately deprovisioned as Empty
vpc-cni addon installed alongside Auto Mode → stale aws-cni binary on nodes conflicted with Auto Mode's native CNI → FailedCreatePodSandBox errors even after nodes registered
The true root cause was #1. Issues #2 and #3 were pre-existing misconfigurations that surfaced during remediation attempts.

