Prevention
Mandatory IAM Policies for EKS Auto Mode
When creating an EKS Auto Mode cluster, AmazonEKSClusterRole must have all 4 policies attached. Missing any one will cause pods or volumes to fail.

Policy	Key Permissions Granted	Symptom if Missing
AmazonEKSClusterPolicy	Core EKS cluster management (DescribeCluster, CreateNodegroup, etc.)	Cluster fails to create or update
AmazonEKSComputePolicy	Auto Mode node provisioning (RunInstances, TerminateInstances, etc.)	Auto Mode cannot launch or terminate nodes
AmazonEKSNetworkingPolicy	Pod IP assignment (AssignPrivateIpAddresses, CreateNetworkInterface, AttachNetworkInterface, etc.)	All pods stuck Pending — IPAM controller gets 403 on AssignPrivateIpAddresses
AmazonEKSBlockStoragePolicy	EBS volume management (CreateVolume, AttachVolume, etc.)	PersistentVolumeClaims fail to bind — EBS CSI driver gets 403
Addons Incompatible with EKS Auto Mode
Addon	Conflict	Action
vpc-cni	Auto Mode has a built-in CNI. Installing vpc-cni deploys the aws-node daemonset and writes the aws-cni binary to /opt/cni/bin on nodes. Auto Mode nodes then invoke aws-cni for pod networking instead of the native CNI, causing FailedCreatePodSandBox.	Delete: aws eks delete-addon --cluster-name <cluster> --addon-name vpc-cni. Then recycle nodes: kubectl delete nodeclaim --all
aws-ebs-csi-driver	Auto Mode includes a built-in EBS CSI driver. Installing the addon creates a duplicate driver that can conflict with volume attachment.	Remove if using Auto Mode storage. Use the built-in driver instead.
kube-proxy (self-managed)	Auto Mode manages kube-proxy natively. A self-managed kube-proxy daemonset can conflict with Auto Mode's network rules.	Use the EKS-managed kube-proxy addon or remove it — Auto Mode handles this automatically.
IAM Credential Propagation Delay
After attaching an IAM policy to a role, assumed-role sessions created before the attachment will not pick up the new policy until their session token expires — typically 60–90 seconds for EKS Auto Mode controller sessions.

Do not re-apply the fix or recycle nodes during this window
The IPAM controller will retry automatically and succeed once its session refreshes
Confirm it worked by watching CloudTrail AssignPrivateIpAddresses events — the first event with no errorCode confirms the session has refreshed
