why it don't work with node group
Amazon Q
Great question. EKS Auto Mode and managed node groups are fundamentally different compute models — the runbook above applies only to Auto Mode clusters. Here's why the same issue would manifest differently (or not at all) with node groups:

Why AmazonEKSNetworkingPolicy is NOT needed with node groups
Auto Mode vs Managed Node Groups — key architectural difference
Aspect	EKS Auto Mode	Managed Node Groups
Who assigns pod IPs	EKS IPAM controller running as AmazonEKSClusterRole	aws-node daemonset (vpc-cni) running on each node as AmazonEKSNodeRole
Where ec2:AssignPrivateIpAddresses is called from	Cluster control plane role	Node IAM role (AmazonEKSNodeRole)
Policy that grants it	AmazonEKSNetworkingPolicy on the cluster role	AmazonEKS_CNI_Policy on the node role
CNI binary	Built into Auto Mode — no daemonset	aws-node daemonset deployed on every node
vpc-cni addon	Incompatible — conflicts with built-in CNI	Required — this IS the CNI
NodeClaims / Karpenter	Used by Auto Mode to manage nodes	Not used — ASG manages nodes
With node groups, the equivalent issue would look like this
If you saw pods stuck Pending on a node group cluster, the diagnosis path is completely different:

# Step 1 — Check aws-node daemonset is healthy (this is the CNI on node groups)
kubectl get pods -n kube-system -l k8s-app=aws-node

# Step 2 — Check the NODE role (not the cluster role) for CNI permissions
aws iam list-attached-role-policies --role-name <AmazonEKSNodeRole-name>
# Must include: AmazonEKS_CNI_Policy  ← this is the node group equivalent

# Step 3 — Check vpc-cni addon is installed (required on node groups, unlike Auto Mode)
aws eks list-addons --cluster-name <cluster>
# vpc-cni MUST be present on node group clusters

# Step 4 — Check node group is healthy
aws eks describe-nodegroup --cluster-name <cluster> --nodegroup-name <nodegroup>
# Look for: status = ACTIVE, all desired nodes in READY state

Run in CloudShell
Side-by-side: same symptom, different fix
Symptom	Auto Mode fix	Node group fix
Pods stuck Pending, no IPs assigned	Attach AmazonEKSNetworkingPolicy to AmazonEKSClusterRole	Attach AmazonEKS_CNI_Policy to AmazonEKSNodeRole
FailedCreatePodSandBox	Remove vpc-cni addon + recycle nodes	Reinstall / repair vpc-cni addon
Nodes not registering	Fix access entry type to EC2_LINUX	Check node group IAM role trust policy and AmazonEKSWorkerNodePolicy
AssignPrivateIpAddresses denied	Cluster role missing AmazonEKSNetworkingPolicy	Node role missing AmazonEKS_CNI_Policy
The bottom line: On node groups, vpc-cni is the CNI and it runs on the node using the node's IAM role — so IP assignment permissions live on AmazonEKSNodeRole, not AmazonEKSClusterRole. The AmazonEKSNetworkingPolicy and the built-in IPAM controller are Auto Mode-only concepts that simply don't exist in the node group architecture.
